.TH "ELEMENT.POINTEROVER_EVENT" "3JS" "July 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.pointerover_event \- Element: pointerover event
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[CR]pointerover\f[R] event is fired when a pointing device is
moved into an element\(cqs hit test boundaries.
.PP
\f[CR]pointerover\f[R] events have the same problems as
\f[CR]mouseover\f[R].
If the target element has child elements, \f[CR]pointerout\f[R] and
\f[CR]pointerover\f[R] events fire as the pointer moves over the
boundaries of these elements too, not just the target element itself.
Usually, \f[CR]pointerenter\f[R] and \f[CR]pointerleave\f[R] events\(cq
behavior is more sensible, because they are not affected by moving into
child elements.
.SH SYNTAX
Use the event name in methods like \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R], or set
an event handler property.
.IP
.EX
addEventListener(\(dqpointerover\(dq, (event) => { })

onpointerover = (event) => { }
.EE
.SH EVENT TYPE
A \f[CR]PointerEvent\f[R].
Inherits from \f[CR]Event\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
Using \f[CR]addEventListener()\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.addEventListener(\(dqpointerover\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer moved in\(dq);
});
.EE
.PP
Using the \f[CR]onpointerover\f[R] event handler property:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] para = document.querySelector(\(dqp\(dq);

para.onpointerover = (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqPointer moved in\(dq);
};
.EE
